# Skill: SCP file transfer

Use the helper `scripts/scp_transfer.sh` to copy a **source path** to a
**destination path** on (or from) a target server.

## How to run

```bash
# Upload: SRC is local, DST is on the remote host (default direction)
scripts/scp_transfer.sh -H <user>@<host> --to-remote -s <local-src> -d <remote-dst>

# Download: SRC is on the remote host, DST is local
scripts/scp_transfer.sh -H <user>@<host> --from-remote -s <remote-src> -d <local-dst>
```

Key options: `-H/--host`, `-u/--user`, `-p/--port` (default 22),
`-i/--identity <key>`, `-J/--jump <bastion>`, `-s/--src`, `-d/--dst`,
`-r/--recursive`, `-C/--compress`, `--dry-run`.

Direction flags: `--to-remote` (default) or `--from-remote`.
Defaults can come from env vars: `SSH_HOST`, `SSH_USER`, `SSH_PORT`, `SSH_IDENTITY`.

## Agent guidance

- **Always pass both `--src` and `--dst` explicitly**; this skill is for a given
  source path and destination path.
- Recursion is auto-enabled when uploading a local directory; pass `-r`
  explicitly when downloading a remote directory.
- Use `--dry-run` to show the exact `scp` command before overwriting anything at
  the destination.
- The script fails early if a local upload source does not exist, so a failure
  usually means a wrong local path — check it rather than retrying.
- Mind trailing slashes on directories, same as normal `scp`/`cp` semantics.

## Examples

```bash
# upload a single MLIR file
scripts/scp_transfer.sh -H dev@10.0.0.5 -i ~/.ssh/id_rsa --to-remote \
  -s ./model.mlir -d /work/in/model.mlir

# download a results directory
scripts/scp_transfer.sh -H dev@10.0.0.5 --from-remote -r \
  -s /work/out -d ./out
```
